            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>After Caesar's Assassination</strong><br>\nThe assassination of Julius Caesar in 44 BC was a turning point for Antony. He delivered a stirring eulogy at Caesar's funeral, which turned public opinion against the assassins. Seizing the moment, Antony attempted to position himself as Caesar's rightful successor, initiating a struggle for power within the fragmented Roman state. However, his ambition faced serious challenges from Caesar's adopted son, Octavian, and other rivals.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Second Triumvirate</strong><br>\nIn an effort to consolidate power and counter threats, Antony formed the Second Triumvirate with Octavian and Lepidus in 43 BC. This alliance was legalized by the Lex Titia and gave the trio dictatorial powers to pursue Caesar's assassins. The Triumvirate divided the Roman Republic's territories among themselves, marking the end of the republic and the beginning of the empire. Antony took control over the eastern provinces, which allowed him to amass wealth and military strength.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Relationship with Cleopatra</strong><br>\nAntony's involvement with Cleopatra VII of Egypt began as a political alliance but grew into a legendary romance that influenced historical events. Cleopatra bore him three children and provided him with financial and military support. Their relationship entwined Egypt's fortunes with Antony's, complicating his role in Roman politics and leading to conflicts with Octavian, who used Antony's affair to undermine his authority in Rome.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
