          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Battle of Actium</strong><br>\nThe rivalry between Antony and Octavian escalated into a civil war, culminating in the naval Battle of Actium in 31 BC. Antony and Cleopatra combined their forces to take on Octavian's navy. The battle was a decisive victory for Octavian and a crushing defeat for Antony. His alliance with Cleopatra, which had once strengthened his position, now contributed to his downfall as it alienated him from Rome.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Antony's Final Days</strong><br>\nAfter the defeat at Actium, Antony's support rapidly eroded. With few options left, he retreated to Egypt with Cleopatra. As Octavian's forces closed in, Antony was falsely informed that Cleopatra had died. In despair, believing his partner was dead and his cause lost, Antony took his own life. Only later did he discover that Cleopatra was still alive, making his suicide a tragic error based on misinformation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Marc Antony</strong><br>\nDespite his military skills and early political successes, Antony is often remembered for his relationship with Cleopatra and his defeat by Octavian. After his death, Octavian became the first Roman emperor, taking the name Augustus. Antony's legacy is complex, marked by both his personal failings and his significant impact on the Roman world, which continued to resonate long after his death. His life and demise signify the tumultuous transition from the Roman Republic to the Roman Empire.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
